00:03:39.000 --> 00:04:16.000
defenses, it's very revealing. When an incident like this occurs, we want to hear the story of what happened. We want to know what was going on in the mind of the shooter and the mind of the victim. Was Terence Croucher truly a threat? Did Betty Shelby dislike black people? What clues explain the behavior of these individuals? We home in, dig for facts, and look for psychological explanations. But what if there's another way to think about what happened, one that has less to do with the individuals involved, and more to do with the context in which the shooting occurred?
辩护，是非常具有启示意义的。当发生这样的事件时，我们想听听事情经过的故事。我们想知道开枪者和受害者的心中都在想些什么。特伦斯·克劳彻真的构成威胁吗？贝蒂·谢尔比讨厌黑人吗？什么线索能解释这些人的行为？我们深入研究、挖掘事实并寻找心理学解释。但是，如果我们有另一种方式来思考所发生的事情呢？这种方式与涉事个人的关系较小，而与枪击事件发生时的背景关系更大？

9
00:04:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
What we're discovering here is that the individual mind sits in society, and the connection between mind and society is an extremely important one that should not be forgotten. Individual behavior and the mind of the village this week on Hidden Brain. I'm Maserine Banaji. Maserine is a psychology professor at Harvard. She's made a career out of studying the invisible. For the past 30 years, I've been interested in studying those aspects of our minds that are hidden from our own conscious awareness. Maasareen's interest began in graduate school.
我们在这里发现的是，个人思维植根于社会之中，思维与社会之间的联系极其重要，不容忽视。本周在《隐藏的大脑》中探讨个体行为与群体的思维。我是玛扎琳·巴纳吉。玛扎琳是哈佛大学的心理学教授。她毕生致力于研究无形的事物。在过去的30年里，我一直对研究我们意识中隐藏的那些思维层面感兴趣。玛扎琳的兴趣始于研究生院。

10
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:48.000
She was teaching psychology at Yale and looking for a way to measure people's biases. There was debate over the right scientific method to do this. You could simply ask people their views, but because prejudice is a sensitive topic, you often don't get anything. You couldn't walk up to somebody and say, do you agree that Italians are lazy and have them say yes or no? They'll just refuse to answer that question. A deep-seated discomfort about discussing prejudice was one hurdle for a researcher
当时她在耶鲁大学教心理学，并寻找一种衡量人们偏见的方法。关于实现这一目标的正确科学方法曾引发过争论。你可以直接询问人们的观点，但由于偏见是一个敏感话题，你往往得不到任何结果。你不能走到某个人面前说：“你同意意大利人很懒吗？”然后让他们回答是或否。他们只会拒绝回答这个问题。对于研究人员来说，对讨论偏见根深蒂固的不适感是一个障碍

11
00:05:48.000 --> 00:06:21.000
looking to study the phenomenon. Maserine realized there was another barrier. What if some forms of prejudice are so deeply buried that people don't even realize they harbor such bias? Perhaps we behave in ways that are not known to our own conscious awareness, that we are being driven to act in certain ways not because we are explicitly prejudiced, but because we make Harry in our heads the thumbprint of the culture. Was there a way to decipher this thumbprint and expose people's hidden biases?
想要研究这一现象的研究人员面临的一个障碍。玛扎琳意识到还有另一个障碍。如果某些形式的偏见埋藏得如此之深，以至于人们甚至没有意识到自己怀有这种偏见呢？也许我们的行为方式连我们自己的意识都不知道，我们之所以会被驱使以某种方式行事，不是因为我们有显性的偏见，而是因为我们在脑海中烙下了文化的指纹。有没有一种方法可以解读这个指纹并暴露出人们隐藏的偏见呢？

12
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Eventually Maserine, with the help of her, Tony Greenwald, and then graduate student, Brian Nosek, developed a simple, ingenious test. It's called the implicit association test or the IAT. It's based on the way we group things in our minds. When you say bread, my mind will easily think butter, but not something unrelated to it. Like say, a hammer. Our brains it turns out make associations and these associations can reveal important things about the way we think.
最终，玛扎琳在托尼·格林沃尔德以及当时的研究生布赖恩·诺塞克的帮助下，开发了一个简单而巧妙的测试。它被称为内隐联结测验，简称IAT。它基于我们在脑海中对事物进行分组的方式。当你提到面包时，我的大脑会很容易联想到黄油，而不会联想到与之无关的东西。比如，一把锤子。事实证明，我们的大脑会建立联结，而这些联结可以揭示关于我们思维方式的重要信息。

13
00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:14.000
So the way the IAT works is to simply ask people to sort things. So imagine that you're given a deck of playing cards and you're asked to sort all the red cards to the left and all the black cards to the right. I'll predict that it will take you about 20 seconds to run through that deck. Next, Mazarin says, shuffle the deck and resort the cards.
因此，IAT的工作原理就是简单地要求人们对事物进行分类。所以想象一下，你拿到了一副扑克牌，要求你把所有的红牌分到左边，把所有的黑牌分到右边。我预测你大概需要20秒钟就能过完这副牌。接着，玛扎琳说，洗牌并重新对牌进行分类。

14
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:38.000
This time, I'd like you to put all the spades and the diamonds to one side and the clubs and the hearts to the other side. And what we'll find is that this will take you nearly twice as long to do. the diamonds to one side and the clubs and the hearts to the other side. And what we'll find is that this will take you nearly twice as long to do. Why? Because a rule that your brain had learned, red and red go together, black and black go together is no longer available to you.
这一次，我希望你把所有的黑桃和方块放在一边，把梅花和红桃放在另一边。我们会发现，你做这件事所需的时间将近两倍。把方块放在一边，把梅花和红桃放在另一边。我们会发现，你做这件事所需的时间将近两倍。为什么？因为你大脑曾经学过的一个规则——红色与红色归为一组，黑色与黑色归为一组——对你不再适用了。

15
00:07:38.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Remember that in both scenarios, you are grouping two suits together. In the first scenario, you are grouping hearts with diamonds and clubs with spades. In the second scenario, you are grouping hearts with clubs and diamonds with spades. Because there's a simple rule for the first task, group red with red and black with black, that task is easy. In the second scenario, you need a fraction of a second to think about each card. You can't follow a simple rule of thumb. Maserine and Tony and Brian had an important insight. These rules of association apply to many subjects, including the way we think about other human beings.
请记住，在这两种情况下，你都是将两种花色归为一组。在第一种情况下，你将红桃与方块、梅花与黑桃归为一组。在第二种情况下，你将红桃与梅花、方块与黑桃归为一组。由于第一个任务有一个简单的规则，即红色与红色归为一组，黑色与黑色归为一组，因此该任务很容易。在第二种情况下，你每看一张牌都需要零点几秒的时间来思考。你无法遵循一个简单的经验法则。玛扎琳、托尼和布赖恩有一个重要的见解。这些联结规则适用于许多主题，包括我们看待其他人类同胞的方式。

16
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:58.000
So they created a new sorting task. Sort for me, faces of black people, and bad things together, words like devil and bomb and vomit and awful and failure. Sort those on one side of the playing deck. On the other side, put the faces of white people, and words like love and peace, enjoy and sunshine and friendly and so on to the other side. This turns out to be pretty easy for us to do because as my colleagues and I will argue, the association of white and good and black and bad has been made for us in our culture.
因此，他们创造了一个新的分类任务。为我把黑人的面孔和不好的事物归为一组，比如魔鬼、炸弹、呕吐物、糟糕和失败这些词。把这些放在扑克牌的一侧。在另一侧，放上白人的面孔，以及爱、和平、享受、阳光、友好等词语放在另一侧。事实证明，这对我们来说很容易做到，正如我和我的同事们所争论的那样，在我们的文化中，白人与美好、黑色与糟糕的联结已经为我们做好了。

17
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:35.000
The test doesn't end there. After sorting white and good into one group and black and bad into another, you now have to do it again, this time grouping black with good and white with bad. And when you try to do that and when I try to do that, the data show that we will slow down, that we can't do it quite as fast because black and good are not practice responses for us. They're not habitual responses for us. We have to exert control to make that happen because it doesn't come naturally and easily to us. That's the IAT.
测试并没有就此结束。在将白色和美好归为一组、黑色和糟糕归为另一组之后，你现在必须再做一次，这次是将黑色与美好归为一组，将白色与糟糕归为一组。当你试图这样做时，当我试图这样做时，数据表明我们会慢下来，我们无法做得那么快，因为黑色和美好对我们来说不是经过练习的反应。它们对我们来说不是习惯性的反应。我们必须施加控制才能让它发生，因为它对我们来说并不是自然而然、轻而易举的。这就是IAT。

18
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:58.000
By the way, if you're wondering whether the order of the test makes a difference, it doesn't. The researchers have presented tests to volunteers in different ways. It doesn't make a difference if you ask people to first group black with bad or first ask them to group black with good. In both cases, people are faster to associate white faces with positive words and black faces with negative words.
顺便说一句，如果你想知道测试的顺序是否有影响，答案是没有。研究人员以不同的方式向志愿者进行了测试。无论你是先要求人们将黑色与糟糕归为一组，还是先要求他们将黑色与美好归为一组，都没有区别。在这两种情况下，人们都能更快地将白人面孔与积极的词汇联系起来，将黑人面孔与消极的词汇联系起来。

19
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:33.000
Mazarin thinks the IAT is measuring a form of bias that is implicit or unconscious. Mazarin herself has taken the IAT many times. To her dismay, the test shows she has robust levels of unconscious bias. My brain simply could not make the association of black with good as quickly as I could make the association of white with good. And that told me something, it told me, it's not the IAT that screwed up, it's my head that screwed up. Because the IAT is a timed test, the results can be precisely measured.
玛扎琳认为，IAT正在测量一种隐性的或无意识的偏见形式。玛扎琳本人也多次参加了IAT测试。令她沮丧的是，测试显示她拥有相当程度的无意识偏见。我的大脑无法像将白色与美好联系起来那样迅速地将黑色与美好联系起来。这告诉我一些事情，它告诉我，出问题的不是IAT，而是我的脑袋。由于IAT是一个计时测试，因此可以精确测量结果。

20
00:10:33.000 --> 00:11:06.000
Implicit bias in other words can be quantified. Most psychological tests are only available in the lab, but Mazarin and her colleagues decided to do something radical. They put their test on the internet. You can find it today at implicit.harvard.edu. Millions of people have taken this test. The data has been collected, shared, disseminated. The IAT is widely considered today to be the most influential test of unconscious bias.
换句话说，内隐偏见是可以被量化的。大多数心理学测试只能在实验室里进行，但玛扎琳和她的同事们决定做一件激进的事情。他们把测试搬到了互联网上。你今天可以在 implicit.harvard.edu 上找到它。数百万人参加了这项测试。数据已被收集、分享、传播。今天，IAT被广泛认为是最具影响力的无意识偏见测试。

21
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:42.000
As Maserine and Tony and Brian were developing the IAT, other researchers were developing different ways to measure bias. Psychologist Joshua Corral found himself diving into the field shortly after a black man was shot and killed in New York City in 1999. His name, Amadou Diallo. Diallo was standing unarmed on the front front stoop of his apartment building in the police thought he looked suspicious and they approached him and they ended up shooting him and the question that everybody was asking and this was I mean something that people you know across
