And my concern is that when I'm an old woman, that I will look back at this time and think, why didn't I do something about this because I don't believe this training is going to do anything? In Mausoleen's view, you can't easily erase implicit bias because you can't erase the effect of the culture when people are living day in and day out in that same culture. But she and others argue that there might be ways to prevent such biases from influencing your behavior.
而我的担忧是，当我成为一个老太太时，我会回首这段时光并想：“我当时为什么不做点什么呢，因为我不相信这种培训会有任何用处？”在玛扎琳看来，你无法轻易消除内隐偏见，因为当人们日复一日地生活在同一种文化中时，你无法消除文化的影响。但她和其他人认为，或许有办法阻止此类偏见影响你的行为。

48
00:26:27.000 --> 00:27:03.000
Let's return to psychologist Joshua Correll. Remember he created the active shooter video game that found test takers were more likely to shoot black targets rather than white ones. Many of Joshua's initial test takers were students. Eventually, he decided to see what would happen if police officers took the test. So he went to the Denver police. We brought down a bunch of laptops and button boxes, a bunch of electronic equipment that we were using to do this study, and we would set it up in their roll call room. And it was just complete chaos and really, really fun. And some of the police really wanted
让我们回到心理学家约书亚·科雷尔。还记得他创造了那个“活跃枪手”电子游戏吗？该游戏发现测试者更有可能射击黑人目标而不是白人目标。约书亚最初的许多测试者都是学生。最终，他决定看看如果警察参加测试会发生什么。于是他去找了丹佛警察局。我们带去了一堆笔记本电脑和按键盒，一堆用于开展这项研究的电子设备，然后我们把它安装在他们的点名室里。那简直是一片混乱，但真的、真的非常有趣。有些警察根本

49
00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:24.000
nothing to do with us, but a huge number of them volunteered and they wanted to talk with us afterwards. At first, the police officers performed exactly the same as everyone else. Their levels of implicit bias were about the same as laypeople who take in the test, both in response times and in mistakes. But when it came to the actual shooting of targets,
不想跟我们扯上关系，但有大量的人自愿参加，而且他们在事起之后还想跟我们交流。起初，警察的表现和其他所有人完全一样。他们的内隐偏见水平与参加测试的普通人差不多，无论是在反应时间还是在错误率上。但当谈到对目标的实际射击时，

50
00:27:24.000 --> 00:28:06.000
the police were very different. The police officers did not show a bias in who they actually shot. Those early test takers, college students and other laypeople displayed their bias on response time, mistakes, and who they shot. Not the police. But whereas those stereotypes may influence the behavior of the college students and of you and me, the police officers are somehow able to exert control. So even though the stereotype, say, of threat may come to mind, the officer can overcome that stereotype and respond based on the information that's actually present in the scene rather than information that the officer is bringing to it through his or her stereotypes.
警察的表现大不相同。警察在究竟射击谁的问题上没有表现出偏见。那些早期的测试者——大学生和其他普通人在反应时间、错误和射击对象上都表现出了他们的偏见。警察则没有。尽管这些刻板印象可能会影响大学生以及你我的行为，但警察不知何故能够施加控制。因此，尽管诸如“威胁”之类的刻板印象可能会浮现在脑海中，但警官可以克服这种刻板印象，并根据现场实际存在的信息做出反应，而不是根据他或她通过刻板印象带入现场的信息。

51
00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:43.000
Joshua wondered whether there were certain factors that might keep police officers from exerting this kind of cognitive control over their biases. He found, among other things, that sleep made a difference. Those who were getting less sleep were more likely to show racial bias in their decisions to shoot. And again, that's just consistent with this idea that they are, they might be able to exert control, to use cognitive resources to avoid showing stereotypic bias in their decisions, but when those resources are compromised, they can't do it. They could be compromised in a variety of ways.
约书亚想知道，是否存在某些因素可能会阻止警察对他们的偏见施加这种认知控制。他发现，除了别的因素之外，睡眠会产生影响。睡眠较少的人在做出开枪决定时更有可能表现出种族偏见。再说一次，这与这种观点完全一致，即他们可能能够施加控制，利用认知资源来避免在决策中表现出刻板印象偏见，但当这些资源受到损害时，他们就无法做到这一点。它们可能会以各种方式受到损害。

52
00:28:43.000 --> 00:29:21.000
